Dear List,

I am working on a hierarchical SUR model and was hoping to get feedback on a
much-simplified version. The Stan SUR model below replicates the results of the
first example shown at the UCLA Stata site <http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/stata/webbooks/reg/chapter4/statareg4.htm> (section 4.5.1 -- example R code is
attached, as is a dump file for the data).

Performance for this simple model/dataset is good enough, but when I take the
model to my data (50000+ obs, 9000+ individuals, in up to 7 equations), things
turn strange (e.g., greater than 48 hours of run time on a high-performance cluster,
very little or no mixing). Now, it's quite possible (even likely) that I have
something wrong in my more complicated model, but I wanted to see if the list
had any comments on this simplified version before I bother you all with the
ugly one. For instance, if my correlation matrices are never more than 7x7, do I
need to go the cholesky route?

Thanks for your time.
~Jason

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Seemingly Unrelated Regression
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

data {
  int<lower=1> N; // number of observations
  int<lower=2> P; // number of equations

  vector[P] x[N]; // first covariate
  vector[P] z[N]; // second covariate
  vector[P] y[N]; // outcomes
}
parameters {
  vector[P] alpha;
  vector[P] beta_x; // first coef.
  vector[P] beta_z; // second coef.

  vector<lower=0>[P] sigma;
  corr_matrix[P] Sigma;
}
model {
  matrix[P,P] Omega;
  Omega <- quad_form_diag(Sigma, sigma);

  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);
  Sigma ~ lkj_corr(2.0);

  alpha  ~ normal(0, 10);
  beta_x ~ normal(0, 10);
  beta_z ~ normal(0, 10);

  {
    vector[P] theta[N];

    for(n in 1:N)
      theta[n] <- alpha + (x[n] .* beta_x) + (z[n] .* beta_z);

    y ~ multi_normal(theta, Omega);
  }
}

Dear Bob, many thanks for your reply.
I have been working on the model and tweaked some code. The likelihood of my model is as follows, and relates to network meta-analysis:

for (n in 1:N) {
  mu[n] <- alpha[xstudy[n]] + delta[xstudy[n]]*if_else((xtreat[n]-xbaseline[n]), 1, 0);
  Y[n] ~ multi_normal(mu[n], (S[xstudy[n]]*ar)); //ar is a correlation matrix
}

for (j in 1:Nstudies){
    md[j] <- d[ttreat[j]] - d[tbaseline[j]];
    delta[j] ~ multi_normal(md[j], Sdelta); //Sdelta is a diagonal matrix with a common heterogeneity term
}

>
> Which version of RStan are you using?

I am using RStan 2.6.0. Btw, I discovered that compilation was aborted because the system I am using only allows a certain computation time (it is a high performance system). I changed some settings and now managed to compile the model and retrieve samples from the posterior. Memory requirements remain very high though, for compilation and sampling. The fitted object (with MCMC samples) takes about 2GB.

I have about 6300 missing values in my data, resulting in 6300 unknown parameters + the standard model parameters (I think about 50).

In general, results that come out of the model seem to make sense, I just wonder why fitting this model is computationally so exhaustive. The dataset I am working with is of average size (about 2000 subjects), and I hope to apply these methods for larger data sets.

